<Project
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="A Study of Bodies, Cities, and Cycles of Change"
    description="Architectural studio project exploring the relationship between human bodies, urban environments, and the cyclical nature of change. The research examines three temporal states—present, death, and revival—juxtaposing the idealized forms of Renaissance architecture with the evolving realities of both body and city. Through this lens, the project reflects on how perfection is not a fixed condition but a cycle of decline, adaptation, and renewal."
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Architectural Studio VI"
    courseDescription="Radicle Roots"
    instructor="Christianna Bennett"
    collaborators="Marinie Ben"
    date="Fall 2022"
    location="Rome, Italy"
>
    <section class="main">
        <p>
            Renaissance architecture reflects an idealized vision of the human
            body—proportioned, perfected, and measured against classical notions
            of harmony. Yet the human body is never static; it adapts to its
            environment just as landscapes shift in response to natural forces
            and cultural change. This project examines adaptation across three
            temporal states—present, death, and revival—while juxtaposing the
            idealized “perfection” of Renaissance form with the evolving
            realities of both body and architecture.
        </p>
        <p>
            In the present, human life is abundant, and the city mirrors the
            disparities of its population. Wealthier districts expand outward
            with access to diverse ecological systems, while poorer areas
            compress into dense, survival-driven conditions. At death, the human
            population and pollution reach their peak. The world becomes a
            fractured, war-like artificial landscape where nature has been
            stripped away, and social structures collapse into pack mentalities,
            leaving outsiders to fend for themselves. In revival, the
            environment enters a phase of rehabilitation. Organic matter
            reclaims the artificial, ecosystems regenerate, and life begins to
            flourish once more.
        </p>
        <p>
            Through these three states, the project reflects on how bodies,
            cities, and architectural ideals transform under pressure—revealing
            that perfection is not a fixed condition, but a cycle of decline,
            adaptation, and renewal.
        </p>
    </section>
</Project>
